signal()` from the already-linked libc (no new dependency).\n\nTwo things worth flagging about how I validated this:\n\n- **My first test for it was flaky and I did not trust the green result.** I originally raced a FIFO reader that exited immediately; C reported 141 on one run and 0 on the next. Because the output (~103 bytes) fits entirely in the 64 KiB pipe buffer, the write succeeds unless the read end is *already* gone. The final test is deterministic: `pipe(2)`, give the child the write end as stdout, close the read end *before* spawning.\n- **I confirmed the test can actually fail.** Commenting the fix out produces `C=Some(13), Rust=None`. A passing test that cannot fail would have proven nothing here.\n\n**Bug 2 — buffering discipline (fidelity hardening).** The translation issued 14 write syscalls; C stdio does one buffered write at exit when stdout isn't a TTY, and line-buffers when it is. I found **no input where this changed the compared bytes**, and I've said so plainly in `ERRORS.md` rather than dressing it up as a caught bug — it's fixed because it's observable in principle (if a reader vanished mid-run, the two could emit different partial output). Verified in both modes, including running both binaries under a real PTY (byte-identical).\n\n**C quirks preserved, not \"fixed\":** `bad()` does *not* call `helperBad()` — so `helperGood()` appears in the output and `helperBad()` never does. `helper_bad` is kept defined and uncalled. `printLine`'s `NULL` guard is unreachable (all 8 call sites pass literals), so no test can cover it; I documented that instead of inventing a test that pretends to.\n\n## Gate status\n\n- Both build clean — 0 warnings, 0 errors (from a wiped `target/`)\n- `cargo test`: **25 passed, 0 failed, 0 ignored**, in both debug and `--release`\n- Nothing `#[ignore]`d or skipped (grep-verified: 0 markers)\n- `c_src/` unmodified — sources retain original timestamps; only the required `c_src/build/` was added\n\nOne caveat on coverage honesty: `stdout_to_dev_full_matches` early-returns if `/dev/full` is absent. On this Linux host it did execute the real comparison, but on a platform lacking `/dev/full` it would silently pass without testing anything. `strace` was also blocked by the sandbox, so the syscall-pattern claim in Bug 2 rests on the PTY and pipe comparisons rather than direct syscall counts.","ttft_ms":3336,"ttft_stream_ms":3148,"time_to_request_ms":49,"type":"result","duration_ms":355340,"uuid":"4793f663-5b79-4e7e-85ef-821d93b92cd1","queued_turn_count":0}
